 {
  "claims": [
    {
      "quote": "A model for the mathematical study of immune response to breast cancer is proposed and studied, both analytically and numerically.",
      "text": "The paper asks how tumour-evoked regulatory B cells (tBregs) dynamically influence breast-cancer growth and the immune response."
    },
    {
      "quote": "tBregs protect metastasizing breast cancer cells from immune effector cells by inducing immune suppression mediated by Tregs",
      "text": "The biological mechanism it focuses on is that tBregs, which are evoked by the tumour, promote immune suppression through regulatory T cells (Tregs)."
    },
    {
      "quote": "breast cancer seems to generate tBregs, which in turn increase the proliferation of Tregs, which are responsible for the death of the tumour-lysing NK cells, leading eventually to lung metastasis",
      "text": "In plain terms: breast cancer creates tBregs; tBregs boost Tregs; Tregs kill NK cells; with fewer NK cells the tumour survives and can metastasise."
    },
    {
      "quote": "we consider logistic growth for the breast cancer cell population, while exponential decay of the rest populations.",
      "text": "The model assumes tumour cells grow logistically, while NK cells, Tregs and tBregs decay exponentially in the absence of interactions."
    },
    {
      "quote": "the rate of every interaction between two cell populations is the product of the size of the two populations, i.e. we consider Holling's type I functional response for every interacting population.",
      "text": "All cell-cell interactions are modelled with simple mass-action (Holling type I) terms."
    },
    {
      "quote": "The first term of the right-hand side of equation (1) models the logistic growth of breast cancer cells, while the second term models the death of breast cancer cells due to NK cells.",
      "text": "Tumour cells are killed by NK cells."
    }
  ],
  "mechanism": "The proposed mechanism is a four-population feedback loop: tumour cells (T) activate tBregs (B); tBregs activate Tregs (R); Tregs inhibit NK cells (N); and NK cells kill tumour cells. Thus tBregs indirectly protect the tumour by suppressing the NK-cell attack.",
  "modelEquations": [
    "eq1",
    "eq2",
    "eq3"
  ],
  "question": "What is the dynamical role of tumour-evoked regulatory B cells (tBregs) in promoting breast cancer?",
  "result": {
    "quote": "if tBregs exist in the body, a sufficiently large number of tBregs can cause the breast tumour to reach its carrying capacity, regardless of the rate of NK-induced breast cancer cell death.",
    "text": "The model shows that, without tBregs, the tumour can only reach its carrying capacity if NK-cell killing becomes very weak. With tBregs present, a large enough tBreg population can push the tumour to its carrying capacity even when NK cells are effective. This suggests that depleting tBregs could limit tumour growth and metastasis."
  }
}
